PivApplet, made to attest as a YubiKey 5 does — for the iya-sts client lab's
virtual PIV card ONLY (#2056, umbrella #2055).

Applied by tests/client-lab/build-cards.sh (`git apply`) to PivApplet
(https://github.com/arekinath/PivApplet) at the commit the lab's Dockerfile
pins, 5cb14a9e8d16e92fbad73dcad86a219a9210554f, in the lab image's `cards`
stage. Never in the product image. Like the file it changes, this patch is
under the Mozilla Public License 2.0 (REUSE.toml); the patched file keeps
its licence notice and is marked MODIFIED where it changes.

WHY. Stock PivApplet's attestation is refused by common/piv_attestation.ts
(the #2056 check): its subject is "PIVApplet Attestation 9A", its issuer is
always the fixed name "PIVApplet Attestation" whatever the f9 certificate
says, and it carries no serial (STS-PIV-0007) and no form factor. The lab
also runs STOCK PivApplet (bin/lab-piv-card stock) so that refusal stays
asserted. The verifier is not changed for either.

WHAT CHANGES, each field as the YubiKey documentation gives it:

  * subject "CN=YubiKey PIV Attestation <slot>", the slot in lower-case hex
    — Yubico, "PIV Attestation" (developers.yubico.com/PIV/Introduction/
    PIV_attestation.html): the attestation certificate's subject;
  * issuer = the f9 attestation certificate's own subject, copied as DER —
    the same page: the slot certificate is signed by, and so names as its
    issuer, the f9 certificate; the fixed name only while f9 still holds
    its self-signed start certificate;
  * 1.3.6.1.4.1.41482.3.3, firmware, three octets: 5.4.3 (a released
    YubiKey 5 firmware; also what GET VERSION answers) — Yubico, "Yubico
    PIV attestation extensions" (developers.yubico.com/PIV/Introduction/
    Yubico_extensions.html, the OID arc listed on .../Yubico_Attestation_OID);
  * 1.3.6.1.4.1.41482.3.7, the serial, a DER INTEGER — the same page. The
    card's 4-byte serial (GET SERIAL, INS F8) is now positive and of eight
    decimal digits (0x01000000-0x03FFFFFF), as a YubiKey 5's is, drawn from
    the card's own RandomData (the platform SecureRandom under jcardsim), so
    every card has its own;
  * 1.3.6.1.4.1.41482.3.8, PIN and touch policy, two octets: unchanged (the
    slot's PIN policy, touch "never" — a virtual card has nothing to touch);
  * 1.3.6.1.4.1.41482.3.9, the form factor, one octet: 0x01, USB-A Keychain
    — the same page.

NOT ADDED: 1.3.6.1.4.1.41482.3.10 (FIPS). The verifier reads it, but a
YubiKey 5 that is not a FIPS model does not carry it, and the FIPS mark of
a FIPS model is read from the f9 certificate, which the lab makes.

